Chapter: RtcGuarded
  cites Works chapter GopRtc

 The CMOS clock updates once a second, and while it does its time
 registers read as garbage. This test is the local stand-in for a real
 board: codex-vm now models the update cycle honestly, so the hazard is
 reproducible here instead of only at the far end of a flash, a walk,
 and a photograph.

 Two readers race the same clock. The unguarded one -- read the seconds
 register and believe it -- is expected to return values that are not
 seconds. The guarded one waits out Update-In-Progress, reads twice, and
 rejects anything outside 0..59; it must NEVER return a bad value, and it
 must still be able to tell the time.

 The pass condition is the guarded reader's: zero bad reads, and a real
 second recovered. What is NOT negotiable is that the guarded reader never
 once hands back a number that a clock cannot produce.

 The unguarded count is not printed at all, and that is a correction. It
 used to print `unguarded-bad-seen=y` or `n`, and the .expected pinned
 `y` -- which asserted it, whatever the paragraph above it said. The
 harness compares the whole output against .expected and has no channel
 for a line that is shown but not checked, so a varying line cannot be
 printed here at all. Measured over four runs on codex-vm it came back
 y, y, n, n: a coin flip sitting in the battery, failing half the release
 tallies for a reason that is not a defect. The probe still RUNS, because
 running it is what drags the clock across a tick and exercises the
 hazard for the guarded reader to survive; only its verdict is gone.

 We say:

Section: Probes

 The unguarded probe runs until the clock TICKS -- until a guarded read
 reports a different second than the one it started on. That spans a whole
 second boundary, and the update window lives inside every one of them.

 That was once written here as "so the hazard cannot be missed by getting
 lucky with timing". It can be missed, and the four runs above are the
 measurement. Spanning the window is not the same as catching a value out
 of range: a read taken mid-update returns whatever the chip has half
 written, and half-written is often still a number under sixty. Crossing
 the boundary makes the hazard LIKELY, not certain, which is why its
 verdict is no longer a pass condition.

 The unguarded reads must run in BURSTS. A guarded read waits the update
 out, so a guarded call between every unguarded one serialises the hazard
 away: the very next unguarded read lands just after the window and can
 never be caught by it. That is a trap worth stating, because a test that
 quietly prevents the failure it is looking for reports a clean bill of
 health for a broken system -- which is how this whole class of bug got as
 far as a hundred flashes.

  unguarded-burst : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  unguarded-burst (n) (acc) = act
    s <- rtc-seconds-unguarded 0
    let hit = if s > 59 then 1 else 0
    in if n <= 0 then acc + hit
    else unguarded-burst (n - 1) (acc + hit)
  end

  bad-unguarded : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  bad-unguarded (start) (fuel) (acc) = act
    burst <- unguarded-burst 20000 0
    g <- rtc-seconds 0
    if fuel <= 0 then acc + burst
    else if g >= 0 & g /= start then acc + burst
    else bad-unguarded start (fuel - 1) (acc + burst)
  end

 A guarded read that lands mid-update returns -1: "I could not read the
 clock", which is the honest answer and the one the caller is built to
 handle. It is not a bad value. A bad value is a number a clock cannot
 produce, and that is what this counts.

  bad-guarded : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  bad-guarded (n) (i) (acc) = act
    s <- rtc-seconds 0
    let hit = if s > 59 then 1 else 0
    in if i >= n then acc + hit
    else bad-guarded n (i + 1) (acc + hit)
  end

Section: Opening

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing
  opening = act
    start <- rtc-seconds 0
    ug <- bad-unguarded start 20000000 0
    g <- bad-guarded 20000 0 0
    now <- rtc-seconds 0
    print-line-uni ("guarded-bad=" & show g)
    print-line-uni ("guarded-reads-a-second=" & (if now >= 0 then "y" else "n"))
  end
